+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer resolution (RFC 6901)
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### User Story 1 - Read a value by pointer (P1)
+A service author gives a JSON document and a JSON Pointer string and gets back the referenced value.
+Acceptance:
+1. `""` returns the whole document.
+2. `"/foo/0"` on `{"foo":["bar","baz"]}` returns `"bar"`.
+3. `"/"` refers to the key `""`; `"/a~1b"` to key `a/b`; `"/m~0n"` to key `m~n`.
+4. A referenced `null`, `false`, `0` or `""` is returned as a value (not treated as missing).
+
+### User Story 2 - Fail loudly on bad pointers (P1)
+1. A non-empty pointer not starting with `/` throws.
+2. `~` not followed by `0` or `1` throws.
+3. A pointer that does not resolve (missing key, out-of-range index, `-`, descending into a scalar) throws.
+
+### Edge cases
+- Array indexes: only `0` or digits without leading zeros; `01`, `+1`, `1.0`, `-1`, `length` do not resolve.
+- Only own properties resolve: `/__proto__`, `/constructor`, `/toString` throw on plain objects.
+- Escape decoding order: `~1` then `~0` (so `~01` is the key `~1`).
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-001 `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` is exported from `src/index.ts`.
+- FR-002 Syntax and evaluation follow RFC 6901 sections 3 and 4.
+- FR-003 Invalid syntax and unresolvable pointers throw an `Error`.
+- FR-004 The URI fragment representation (section 6) is out of scope; the string form only.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- All RFC 6901 section 5 examples return the specified values.
+- Every invalid or unresolvable case above throws.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Documents are plain parsed JSON (objects, arrays, scalars). No dependencies are added.
